# The fake Graph server of the local demo. It stands in for the real Cloud API: it validates what it receives, records
# everything, can be told to fail, and (when configured with a callback URL and an app secret) posts signed webhooks
# to the service. Syntax: VS Code "REST Client" / JetBrains HTTP client.

@graph = http://localhost:5103

### 1. Messages the fake server accepted (optionally only for one recipient). Expect: 200
GET {{graph}}/_inspect/messages
# GET {{graph}}/_inspect/messages?to=%2B12025550100

### 2. Every request it received, including rejected ones, with the outcome. Expect: 200
GET {{graph}}/_inspect/requests

### 3. Fail the next two requests with a server error (transient: the service retries). Expect: 201
# Modes: rate-limit, server-error, service-unavailable, invalid-recipient, template-not-found,
#        token-expired, outside-window, parameter-mismatch, drop-after-accept.
# Optional fields: "recipient" (only fail requests to that number), "retryAfterSeconds" (for rate-limit).
# drop-after-accept records the message as accepted, posts its status webhooks, and then breaks the connection
# before the answer: the caller sees a network failure although the message was accepted.
POST {{graph}}/_inspect/failures
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "server-error", "remaining": 2 }

### 4. Active failure rules. Expect: 200
GET {{graph}}/_inspect/failures

### 5. Remove all failure rules. Expect: 204
DELETE {{graph}}/_inspect/failures

### 6. How the next accepted messages to a number end. Expect: 201
# final: read (sent, delivered, read: the default), delivered, sent, failed (with errorCode and errorTitle), none (no webhook).
# "remaining" limits the rule to that many messages. Without it the rule stays until it is deleted.
POST {{graph}}/_inspect/outcomes
Content-Type: application/json

{ "recipient": "+12025550100", "final": "failed", "errorCode": 131047, "errorTitle": "Re-engagement message", "remaining": 1 }

### 7. Active outcome rules. Expect: 200
GET {{graph}}/_inspect/outcomes

### 8. Remove all outcome rules. Expect: 204
DELETE {{graph}}/_inspect/outcomes

### 9. A customer writes to the business number: the fake posts a signed inbound message webhook to the service. Expect: 200
# phoneNumberId selects the tenant that owns the number. The answer reports what the service replied.
POST {{graph}}/_inspect/inbound
Content-Type: application/json

{ "from": "+12025550100", "phoneNumberId": "100200300", "text": "Hello" }

### 10. Every webhook the fake posted, with the HTTP status the service answered. Expect: 200
GET {{graph}}/_inspect/webhooks

### 11. Forget the recorded requests and webhooks. Expect: 204
DELETE {{graph}}/_inspect/messages

### 12. The Graph-style send endpoint itself, called directly: the demo token is accepted. Expect: 200
POST {{graph}}/v1/100200300/messages
Authorization: Bearer dev-only-fake-access-token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messaging_product": "whatsapp",
  "to": "+12025550100",
  "type": "text",
  "text": { "body": "Hello from a direct call" },
  "biz_opaque_callback_data": "demo-callback-data"
}

### 13. A token the fake server reports as expired. Expect: 401
POST {{graph}}/v1/100200300/messages
Authorization: Bearer dev-only-expired-token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messaging_product": "whatsapp",
  "to": "+12025550100",
  "type": "text",
  "text": { "body": "Hello" }
}
